==================================================
Lab 5: SQL Injection UNION Attack - Retrieving Data
from Other Tables
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

This lab builds on everything learned so far. We already know how to find the number of columns a query returns,
and how to find which column accepts text. Now we put both pieces of knowledge together to actually steal real
data from a completely different table in the database, specifically the users table, which holds usernames and
passwords.

Goal: use a UNION attack to pull the username and password of a user out of the users table, and display them on
the product page.

==============================
HOW THIS BUILDS ON EARLIER LABS

In lab three, we found out exactly how many columns the original query returns.
In lab four, we found out which of those columns can safely hold text.
In this lab, instead of putting NULL or a random test string into those columns, we put real column names from
another table, specifically username and password from the users table. This way, instead of just proving a
column can hold text, we are now using it to actually display stolen data.

=============================
THE TECHNIQUE USED HERE

We already know this lab’s query returns two columns, and both of them accept text, since the product title and
the product price are both text-based fields on the page. So instead of testing column types again, we go
straight to injecting a UNION SELECT that pulls username and password directly from the users table.

======================================
WHAT THE PAYLOAD LOOKS LIKE

The category value sent is: a valid category name, followed by a single quote, then UNION SELECT, then username
comma password, then FROM users, then two dashes.

This tells the database to run two queries together: the original one that fetches products, and our own one
that fetches username and password from the users table. Since both results have two columns, and the database
already confirmed string columns, both sets of results are combined and shown together on the same page, as if
the stolen username and password were just another product in the list.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and go to a product category page.
Submit a real category name, followed by a single quote, then UNION SELECT username, password FROM users--
If the number of columns or their types do not match, adjust based on what was learned in the earlier labs.
Once correct, the username and password appear on the page as if they were a regular product, and the lab is
marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

It imports the requests library, used for sending web requests, and the re library, used for searching and
replacing text with patterns.

A session is created so cookies stay consistent across the request.

It asks you to paste the category filter address, and saves it as filter_url.

It sets valid_category to the text Corporate gifts, a real category name that exists in this lab, so the first
part of the original query keeps working normally.

It builds the payload using the valid category name, a single quote, UNION SELECT, username comma password, FROM
users, and two dashes at the end to comment out the rest of the original query.

This payload is placed into the category parameter and sent to the website through the session.

It prints the status code of the response, just to visually confirm the request went through without a server
error.

It then takes the raw HTML of the response and removes entire script and style blocks first. These blocks
contain CSS styling code and JavaScript code, not actual visible text, so removing them first avoids cluttering
the output with code that was never meant to be read as content.

After removing those blocks, it replaces every remaining HTML tag with a newline character. This turns the whole
page from a wall of HTML into separate lines, since tags like the ones for headings, paragraphs, or list items
normally separate pieces of content from each other.

It then goes through every line, strips extra spaces from the start and end of each line, and completely removes
any line that is empty or only contains whitespace. This leaves only lines that actually contain visible
content.

It prints how many lines remain, just to give a quick sense of how much output there is.

Finally, it prints every cleaned line one by one, so you can scroll through and find the username and password
sitting somewhere among the other product names and prices.

=============================
WHY WE STRIP SCRIPT AND STYLE TAGS FIRST

If script and style blocks are not removed before converting tags into newlines, their raw code content (such as
JavaScript logic or CSS rules) gets treated as if it were visible page text. This creates a lot of confusing,
unreadable lines in the output that have nothing to do with the actual page content a visitor would see. Removing
them first keeps the final output focused only on genuinely visible text.

=============================
WHY THIS APPROACH IS KEPT SIMPLE ON PURPOSE

Earlier attempts tried to target specific HTML classes or tags, assuming the page used particular container
names for each product. This failed because the actual class and tag names in the lab’s HTML were different
from what was assumed. Instead of guessing the exact structure every single time, simply converting the whole
page into clean readable text is more reliable, since it does not depend on knowing the exact HTML layout in
advance. The tradeoff is that the output includes everything on the page, not just the stolen data, so you do
need to glance through the lines yourself to spot the result.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The underlying flaw is still the same as all previous labs, user input is joined directly into the SQL query
text. This time, the UNION SELECT is not just testing column count or type, it is directly requesting real
columns from a completely different table, and the database has no way of knowing this was not part of the
original intended query.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries so that user input can never change the structure of the SQL query, including adding
entirely new SELECT statements through UNION.

Other good practices:

Restrict database account permissions so the account used by the web application cannot read tables it has no
business accessing, such as a users table, if the application only ever needs to read a products table.
Avoid exposing raw database query results directly into web page content without filtering what is shown.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

A UNION attack becomes genuinely dangerous once the attacker knows both the column count and which columns
accept text, since both pieces of knowledge are combined to pull real data from any other table.
The users table and its username and password columns are extremely common targets, since gaining valid
credentials often leads to further access.
Keeping the output simple, as plain cleaned text, is sometimes more practical than trying to target exact page
elements, especially when the HTML structure is not known in advance.
